#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054351 :question/id #uuid "3e60b147-6f6c-585b-8daf-d4499f989cef" :question/math-academy-id "e-1069" :question/problem "Simplify $a^{9}\\cdot a^{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{9}\\cdot {a}^{7} & = \\\\ {a}^{9+7} & = \\\\ {a}^{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054352 :question/id #uuid "0dfa49be-7dbf-51b4-bf95-e4994cd769d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1070" :question/problem "Find $n$ such that $x^2\\cdot x^3 \\cdot x^4 = x^n$ is correct." :question/worked-solution "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2+3+4} & = \\\\ {x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $x^{n}=x^9$, so $n=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054353 :question/id #uuid "dbd05bab-1b11-508b-ab4a-f2667a97de28" :question/math-academy-id "e-1071" :question/problem "Simplify $x\\left(x^3 y^5\\right) \\left(x^2 y^9\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Here we have two variables, $x$ and $y.$ We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{3}{y}^{5})({x}^{2}{y}^{9}) & = \\\\ {x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{9} & = \\\\ ({x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})({y}^{5}\\cdot {y}^{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})({y}^{5}\\cdot {y}^{9}) & = \\\\ {x}^{(1+3+2)}\\cdot {y}^{(5+9)} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}{y}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054374 :question/id #uuid "15e9a793-27b4-5345-bbbf-fdea0fab9b9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12795" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $2 + 7p - 3p.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two like terms, $7p$ and $-3p.$ \n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine them into a single term, adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+7p-3p & = \\\\ 2+(7-3)p & = \\\\ 2+4p & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054375 :question/id #uuid "2da17fa4-3fb1-50b9-a317-47c38107ff60" :question/math-academy-id "e-12796" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $5k - 3k + 2k - t.$" :question/worked-solution "We have three like terms, $5k, -3k,$ and $2k.$ \n\nTo collect these like terms, we combine them into a single term, adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k-3k+2k-t & = \\\\ (5-3+2)k-t & = \\\\ 4k-t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054376 :question/id #uuid "d902668d-ffe0-53bb-a360-2e4e472404f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12797" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $6a+1-2a.$" :question/worked-solution "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $6a$ and $-2a$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6a+1-2a &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{6a-2a}_{a \\textrm{ terms}}+ 1\t&\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms: \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\underbrace{6a-2a}+ 1 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(6-2)a + 1 &=\\[5pt]\n\t\t4a + 1&\t\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054377 :question/id #uuid "c6e112dd-4c10-5ce6-87a9-6066a366dd33" :question/math-academy-id "e-12798" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $2.5x+2y-x+1.5y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we group the like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2.5x+2y-x+1.5y &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{2.5x-x}_{x \\textrm{ terms}}+\\underbrace{2y+1.5y}_{y \\textrm{ terms}} &\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\underbrace{2.5x-x}+\\underbrace{2y+1.5y} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(2.5-1)x + (2+1.5)y &=\\[5pt]\n\t\t1.5x + 3.5y\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054378 :question/id #uuid "f0bfc0e7-0db2-501d-a7b5-c7d14378ff85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12802" :question/problem "Simplify $(4+c)\\cdot 3-8.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $3$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(4+c)\\cdot 3-8 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t4\\cdot3+c\\cdot 3-8 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(12)+(3c)-8 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t12+3c-8 &\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t12+3c-8 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t3c +(12-8)&= \\[5pt]\n\t\t3c+4\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054379 :question/id #uuid "0ca08034-c65f-59ec-8ede-9721182eb989" :question/math-academy-id "e-12803" :question/problem "$(-4)(8x+7y) +30y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $(-4)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(-4)(8x+7y)+30y & = \\\\ (-4)\\cdot 8x+(-4)\\cdot 7y+30y & = \\\\ (-32x)+(-28y)+30y & = \\\\ -32x-28y+30y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-32x-28y+30y & = \\\\ -32x+(-28y+30y) & = \\\\ -32x+2y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054380 :question/id #uuid "fdc27ed5-386d-5fd9-bbf7-4847499c6181" :question/math-academy-id "e-12804" :question/problem "Simplify $(5 - y) \\cdot 3 - 10$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $3$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-y)\\cdot 3-10 & = \\\\ 5\\cdot 3+(-y)\\cdot 3-10 & = \\\\ (15)+(-3y)-10 & = \\\\ 15-3y-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15-3y-10 & = \\\\ (15-10)-3y & = \\\\ 5-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054381 :question/id #uuid "c64cd16e-2f9d-5564-8d04-eddcad2c5d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-12859" :question/problem "Find the value of $q$ that satisfies the equation $\\dfrac{q}{4}=-9.$" :question/worked-solution "In the equation, $q$ is being divided by $4.$ To isolate $q$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $4$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{q}{4} &=-9\\[5pt]\n\t\t4\\cdot \\dfrac{q}{4} &=4\\cdot \\left(-9\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36 \\[5pt]\n                \\dfrac{\\cancel{4}q}{\\cancel{4}} &= -36 \\\n\t\tq &=-36\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054382 :question/id #uuid "5fe35108-2fa8-563e-862e-ad27d4ed5334" :question/math-academy-id "e-12860" :question/problem "Find the value of $k$ that satisfies the equation $-\\dfrac{k}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{k}{2}) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $k$ is being divided by $2.$ To isolate $k$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{k}{2} &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\[5pt]\n\t\t2 \\cdot \\dfrac{k}{2} &=2 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{2k}{2} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}k}{\\cancel{2}} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\[5pt]\n\t\tk &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054383 :question/id #uuid "68865bc2-e4e2-53d9-9988-4fa962e04746" :question/math-academy-id "e-12861" :question/problem "Solve the equation $24=-3z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the equation in a way that's a little more familiar to us. We can do this by swapping the left and right-hand sides of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =-3z \\\\ -3z & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-3.$ To isolate $z$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-3$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t-3z &=24 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {-3z} {-3} &= \\dfrac {24} {-3} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\cancel{-3}z} {\\cancel{-3}} &= -8 \\[5pt]\n\t\tz &=-8\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054384 :question/id #uuid "8955a3ce-a161-5dc5-8ec0-c357a0403b46" :question/math-academy-id "e-12862" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3p-7 =20.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $7$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t3p-7 &=20\\\n\t\t3p-7+7 &=20+7 \\\n                3p + 0 &= 27 \\\n\t\t3p &=27\n\t}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3p &=27 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{3p}{3} &=\\dfrac{27}{3} \\[5pt]  \n\t\t\\dfrac{\\cancel{3}p}{\\cancel{3}} &= 9 \\[5pt]  \n\t\tp &=9\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054386 :question/id #uuid "1f5b9030-a5de-5ac2-95c4-2fbaf638cdab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12864" :question/problem "Solve the equation $7-\\dfrac{p}{3} =9.$" :question/worked-solution "After converting the equation to a more familiar form, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n               7-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=9 \\\n    \t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+7 &=9\\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+7-7 &= 9-7 \\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+0 &= 2 \\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n               -\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2 \\\n              (-1) \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{p}{3} \\right) &=(-1) \\cdot 2 \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{p}{3} &=-2 \\\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{p}{\\cancel{3}} &=3 \\cdot (-2) \\   \n\t\tp &=-6\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054387 :question/id #uuid "6ed7ae7f-2591-5747-9fa0-933260aaaebf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12866" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 =\\dfrac 7 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 &=\\dfrac 7 2\\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} - \\dfrac 1 2+\\dfrac 1 2 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\[5pt]\n-\\dfrac{z}{3} + 0 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=\\dfrac 8 2 \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4 \\\n(-1) \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{z}{3} \\right) &= (-1) \\cdot 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{z}{3} &=-4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{z}{\\cancel{3}} &= 3 \\cdot (-4) \\[5pt]   \n\t\tz &=-12\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054393 :question/id #uuid "dad98e52-6c94-5cb9-95f1-0db822fbb44a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12915" :question/problem "Evaluate $11p-2$ for $p=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $p$ with $\\color{blue}4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t11p-2 &= \\\n\t\t11({\\color{blue}4})-2 &= \\\n\t \t44-2 &= \\\n\t \t42 .\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054394 :question/id #uuid "51bb6ac7-5c24-5805-85ba-9c697f4360b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12919" :question/problem "Evaluate $2(3 x-1) +2$ for $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "We replace $x$ with $-3$ in the expression and evaluate the parentheses first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3x-1)+2 & = \\\\ 2(3\\cdot (-3)-1)+2 & = \\\\ 2(-9-1)+2 & = \\\\ 2(-10)+2 & = \\\\ -20+2 & = \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054395 :question/id #uuid "caf5354e-e240-5515-94c7-6c3caa2063af" :question/math-academy-id "e-12920" :question/problem "Find the value of $x - \\dfrac{3}{2}y$ when $x=2$ and $y=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $-2$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{3}{2}y & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-2) & = \\\\ 2+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-2) & = \\\\ 2+3 & = \\\\ 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054396 :question/id #uuid "6ce1d2ce-e99a-5f5c-a698-3f744805ef24" :question/math-academy-id "e-12921" :question/problem "Calculate $(-3)^3 \\div 6$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(-3)^{3}}_{-27}÷6=(-27)÷6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)÷6 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-27)}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27÷3}{6÷3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054397 :question/id #uuid "d4bc50be-7125-55ef-9d29-c69af56e25d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12922" :question/problem "Evaluate $(1-4)^2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\underbrace{1-4}_{-3})^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =(-3)^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(-3)^{2}}_{9}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =9\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\times 1}{1\\times 12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054398 :question/id #uuid "21fccd11-6107-53b8-beb0-d0c39f23bc17" :question/math-academy-id "e-12923" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} & = \\\\ 5÷4 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overbrace{5÷(-2)^{2}}^{\\frac{5}{4}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054403 :question/id #uuid "cc355989-794d-5053-9539-930149bb2019" :question/math-academy-id "e-12977" :question/problem "Expand $3(4x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4x+2) & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 4x+3\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ 12x+6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054404 :question/id #uuid "8cbbff48-a8bb-54c8-8e64-a73b6f83b590" :question/math-academy-id "e-12978" :question/problem "Expand $(2y+7) \\cdot (-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number \n$(-3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t(2y+7) \\cdot (-3)&=\\[5pt]\n\t2y\\cdot(-3)+7\\cdot(-3)&=\\[5pt]\n\t(-6y) + (-21)&=\\[5pt]\n\t-6y-21\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054405 :question/id #uuid "5a98cb1f-a23d-522b-9677-ed4163db76d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12979" :question/problem "Expand $7(5x-3y).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $7\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(5x-3y) & = \\\\ 7\\cdot 5x+7\\cdot (-3y) & = \\\\ (35x)+(-21y) & = \\\\ 35x-21y & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054406 :question/id #uuid "f93a8815-c7ef-51f9-9c22-f297248fdd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12980" :question/problem "Expand $-11(4x - 3y) .$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $(-11)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11(4x-3y) & = \\\\ (-11)\\cdot 4x+(-11)\\cdot (-3y) & = \\\\ (-44x)+(33y) & = \\\\ -44x+33y & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054411 :question/id #uuid "ca811d3a-a7be-536b-b997-dd2355cb00d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12999" :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation $16 + x =10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the left-hand-side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx + 16 = 10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $16$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $16$ from $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 16 &= 10 \\\n    \t\tx + 16 - 16 &= 10 - 16 \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= -6 \\\n\t\tx &= -6\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054412 :question/id #uuid "094ae644-0ce0-51e7-849f-38320770df93" :question/math-academy-id "e-13000" :question/problem "Solve the equation $x-8 =6.$" :question/worked-solution "To isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $8$ from $x$, which is *adding* $8$ to $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx-8 &= 6 \\\n\t\tx-8+8 &= 6+8 \\\n\t\tx+0 &= 14 \\\n\t\tx &= 14\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054413 :question/id #uuid "2d414c47-6dff-54f2-a23a-ee071f8f43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13001" :question/problem "What is the solution to the equation $-15 = p - 12?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\np-12 = -15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $p$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $12$ from $p$, which is *adding* $12$ to $p.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\np-12 \t\t&= -15\\\np-12 + 12 \t&= -15 + 12\\\np+0\t\t\t&=-3\\\np\t\t\t&=-3    \t\t\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054416 :question/id #uuid "14862e2a-37f9-5990-bf41-80ad10b11c30" :question/math-academy-id "e-13004" :question/problem "Factor the following expression: $-5a-35$." :question/worked-solution "Both terms are negative, so we begin by factoring out a negative sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5a - 35 &=  \\\\\n&= - (5a + 35)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue factoring in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(5a,35) = 5$, so\n$- (5a + 35)|=; - (5 \\cdot a + 5 \\cdot 7)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$- (5 \\cdot a + 5 \\cdot 7)|=; - 5(a + 7)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $-5(a+7)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054420 :question/id #uuid "64b6a66a-c061-52e2-a3aa-e54b7542715c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13033" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 = -8.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the fractional term $\\dfrac{3x}{4}$ by adding $5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 &=-8\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5+5 &=-8+5\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}+0 &=-3 \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4} &=-3\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ so we multiply by its reciprocal $\\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x &=-3 \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{4}x &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot (-3) \\[5pt]\n1 x &= - \\dfrac{4}{\\cancel{3} } \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\[5pt]\nx &= -4.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054424 :question/id #uuid "6acd4bf9-8cf9-5a35-a1f5-a9a01766af87" :question/math-academy-id "e-13069" :question/problem "Evaluate $2-\\dfrac{u}{4}$ for $u=\\dfrac43.$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $u$ with $\\dfrac43$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{u}{4} & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{4}u & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{4}{12} & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{6-1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054470 :question/id #uuid "af2c36f6-7258-56b3-aabc-c0736bdfb6a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14209" :question/problem "Evaluate $|x - 6| + 2 x +3$ for $x = -3.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x = -3$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-6|+2x+3 & = \\\\ |-3-6|+2(-3)+3 & = \\\\ |-9|+2(-3)+3 & = \\\\ 9+2(-3)+3 & = \\\\ 9-6+3 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054471 :question/id #uuid "2af15364-dc4d-52c1-a519-02e8568279ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-14212" :question/problem "Evaluate $|2 - x| + 1 + 5|x|$ for $x = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x = 5$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-x|+1+5|x| & = \\\\ |2-5|+1+5|5| & = \\\\ |-3|+1+5(5) & = \\\\ 3+1+25 & = \\\\ 4+25 & = \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054472 :question/id #uuid "c14b3658-bbe4-53f8-a0b6-377b2586f099" :question/math-academy-id "e-14305" :question/problem "Add the polynomials $x^3-x^2 - 2$ and $-2x^3 + 3x^2-x$." :question/worked-solution "We put a plus sign between the two polynomials, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{3} - x^{2} - 2) + (-2x^{3} + 3x^{2} - x) &=  \\\\\nx^{3} - x^{2} - 2 - 2x^{3} + 3x^{2} - x &=  \\\\\nx^{3} - 2x^{3} - x^{2} + 3x^{2} - x - 2 &=  \\\\\n(1 - 2)x^{3} + (-1 + 3)x^{2} - x - 2 &=  \\\\\n(-1)x^{3} + (2)x^{2} - x - 2 &=  \\\\\n&= - x^{3} + 2x^{2} - x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054473 :question/id #uuid "c2e54fec-23da-515b-994b-8fa0601b5a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14307" :question/problem "If $f(x) = 1-2x^3$ and $g(x)=1+3x^3$, then what is $f(x) + g(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "We put a plus sign between the two polynomials, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) + g(x) &=  \\\\\n(1 - 2x^{3}) + (1 + 3x^{3}) &=  \\\\\n- 2x^{3} + 3x^{3} + 1 + 1 &=  \\\\\n(-2 + 3)x^{3} + (1 + 1) &=  \\\\\n(1)x^{3} + (2) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054474 :question/id #uuid "4d680b83-1789-5786-92a5-313073b253c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14310" :question/problem "Subtract the polynomial $1-x-2x^2$ from $x^2+x+6$." :question/worked-solution "We put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute it over the second polynomial, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + x + 6) - (1 - x - 2x^{2}) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + x + 6 - 1 + x + 2x^{2} &=  \\\\\n(1 + 2)x^{2} + (1 + 1)x + (6 - 1) &=  \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} + 2x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054475 :question/id #uuid "4c4aa55e-a77c-58c5-a3f7-74a7cf57eb48" :question/math-academy-id "e-14315" :question/problem "If $f(x) = x^2-6$ and $g(x) =4 - x^2$, what is $f(x)-g(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "We put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute the minus sign, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) - g(x) &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6) - (4 - x^{2}) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 6 - 4 + x^{2} &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + x^{2}) + (-6 - 4) &=  \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} - 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054476 :question/id #uuid "1e0116a2-6f90-5b57-97c1-6a64b45c9cef" :question/math-academy-id "e-14343" :question/problem "Factor the expression $z^2+13z+36$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$z^2+13z+36$.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(36)$:\n\n$$\n(1, 36), (2, 18), (3, 12), (4, 9), (6, 6)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(13)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 36 &= 37 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 18|= 20 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 12|= 15 & \\cdot \\\\ 4 + 9|= 13 & ✓ \\\\ 6 + 6|= 12 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n13z=4z + 9z\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} + 13z + 36 &=  \\\\\n&= z^{2} + 4z + 9z + 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} + 4z + 9z + 36 &=  \\\\\n(z^{2} + 4z) + (9z + 36) &=  \\\\\nz(z + 4) + 9(z + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= (z + 9)(z + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054477 :question/id #uuid "f51ebe90-e58a-5712-84b8-4367ed18d58e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14346" :question/problem "Factor the expression $n^2 - 6n + 8$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$n^2 - 6n + 8$.\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(8)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-6)$ is *negative*.\n\nTherefore, we first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(8)$:\n\n$$\n(-1,-8), \\quad (-2,-4)\n$$\n\nNow we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-6)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-8) &= -9 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-4)|=-6 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-6n=-2n-4n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{2} - 6n + 8 &=  \\\\\n&= n^{2} - 2n - 4n + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{2} - 2n - 4n + 8 &=  \\\\\n(n^{2} - 2n) + (-4n + 8) &=  \\\\\nn(n - 2) - 4(n - 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (n - 4)(n - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054478 :question/id #uuid "41c3e610-0259-59db-9494-3af6f47c7360" :question/math-academy-id "e-14350" :question/problem "Factor the expression $a^2+a-20$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n$a^2+a-20$.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-20)$:\n\n$$\n(1,-20), (-1, 20), (2,-10), (-2, 10), (4,-5), (-4, 5)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-20) &= -19 & \\cdot \\\\ -1 + 20|= 19 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-10)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ -2 + 10|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 4 + (-5)|=-1 & \\cdot \\\\ -4 + 5|= 1 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\na=-4a+5a\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} + a - 20 &=  \\\\\n&= a^{2} - 4a + 5a - 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} - 4a + 5a - 20 &=  \\\\\n(a^{2} - 4a) + (5a - 20) &=  \\\\\na(a - 4) + 5(a - 4) &=  \\\\\n&= (a + 5)(a - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054490 :question/id #uuid "25228be0-9b9e-50b6-9435-b95178ff0734" :question/math-academy-id "e-1540" :question/problem "Simplify $-\\left(-p + 2q - \\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)$." :question/worked-solution "To distribute the negative sign to the terms inside the parentheses, all we have to do is change the sign of each term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (-p + 2q - \\frac{1}{3}) &=  \\\\\n&= p - 2q + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054500 :question/id #uuid "04b471e2-edcd-5f01-8677-e93ddc547a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-18927" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3} + 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3} + 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\sqrt{3} + 1} {\\sqrt{3} + 1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 - 1^2} &= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {3 - 1} &= \\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {2} &= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\\sqrt 3 + 1 &\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054501 :question/id #uuid "0e811568-769f-5e18-9c6c-5594c18330b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18931" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{10-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054502 :question/id #uuid "5a664cc0-30ca-5097-9505-0755963b0f1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-18932" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2} - 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2} - 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{\\sqrt{2}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2}-1)}{(\\sqrt{2}+1)(\\sqrt{2}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(1)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-2+\\sqrt{2}}{2-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-4+\\sqrt{2}}{1} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054504 :question/id #uuid "8a6eb51f-6232-5a08-acf1-136f0d14a4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-191" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nm\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\, .\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054505 :question/id #uuid "cd630daa-4813-5ca8-b3d4-5fe35c4af4fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-192" :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m',$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y)\n\t&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\n\t(2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\n\t\n}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ is \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tm\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4}\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2}{-6}\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, .\n\t\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $m'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\ty-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\ty+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\ty&=3x-5\n}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\ty&=3x-5\\[5pt]\n\t0&=3x-5\\[5pt]\n\tx&=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054512 :question/id #uuid "9f9e4a39-22c9-5bcf-9b34-b3b1459acb44" :question/math-academy-id "e-30" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right]   &= \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)^3\\cdot  \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)\\right)^3\\cdot  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)\\right)^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= (2\\cdot 3 -1)^3\\cdot (3-2)^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= 5^3 \\cdot 1^9\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    & = 125.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054576 :question/id #uuid "6be67098-9840-515b-b0db-e1a0ac9c0cf8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6798" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|-18a|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|18a|\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|18a| & = \\\\ |18\\cdot a| & = \\\\ |18|\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 18\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 18|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054577 :question/id #uuid "0a7c872a-a6f5-543e-835f-4c848f482282" :question/math-academy-id "e-6799" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left|-\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{9}{5c}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|9|}{|5c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|5c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|5|\\cdot |c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{5|c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adde8b3-07d2-55b6-a1f0-3c38ee251bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16u-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488494} {:answer/id #uuid "7c324be0-96e7-5ffe-bcfe-ef9337be7a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18u-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488717} {:answer/id #uuid "a12bce51-2478-5eb3-b82a-2dd61753bcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16u-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488787} {:answer/id #uuid "c86e6225-f0f0-5ec3-9fed-7879c6d560c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18u-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488880} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d7958e-1aae-57c9-9588-d067a948ae50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16u+4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c324be0-96e7-5ffe-bcfe-ef9337be7a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18u-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488717} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f10d8a0-402a-56dc-90f7-09efc71a379e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eba523b6-dd38-519f-9070-b818c12401b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141873" :question/problem "$2u+(5u+v)\\cdot (-4)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $(-4)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}2u+(5u+v)\\cdot (-4) & = \\\\ 2u+5u\\cdot (-4)+v\\cdot (-4) & = \\\\ 2u+(-20u)+(-4v) & = \\\\ 2u-20u-4v & .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u-20u-4v & = \\\\ (2u-20u)-4v & = \\\\ -18u-4v & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e021af68-5d6f-5219-aa63-1cfbae73cd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e021af68-5d6f-5219-aa63-1cfbae73cd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481159} :answer-field/id #uuid "7605857f-6ec1-5c37-83db-3d9633b3952d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77b7fa26-d28f-58ff-acd8-77ebdc7f46c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-212318" :question/problem "If $t=0,$ then $|t-2|-|t+3|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=0$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|t-2|-|t+3| & = \\\\ |0-2|-|0+3| & = \\\\ |-2|-|3| & = \\\\ 2-3 & = \\\\ -1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026f402f-4f08-5e98-9638-1f92ff4899a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}^{18}\\cdot {c}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480114} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3209b9-00a2-5a1a-a7a0-639a1f4a7573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480152} {:answer/id #uuid "81252667-4f78-5e9b-ba67-141d2bb31903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}^{7}\\cdot {c}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480326} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7d415e-0961-5b0a-98e7-925e1bdf7a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}^{14}\\cdot {c}^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480504} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e41fa0-f099-5121-8725-02fe13cbd7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}^{6}\\cdot {c}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee7d415e-0961-5b0a-98e7-925e1bdf7a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}^{14}\\cdot {c}^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480504} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed94865-1e1c-5e1b-820d-0d74db0d957f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5376669b-d519-5bda-b07f-57fecc79f283" :question/math-academy-id "q-22278" :question/problem "Use exponents to rewrite the product $b\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here we have two variables, $b$ and $c.$ We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{7} & = \\\\ {b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6} & = \\\\ ({b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7})\\cdot ({c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7})\\cdot ({c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}) & = \\\\ {b}^{(1+2+4+7)}\\cdot {c}^{(3+5+6)} & = \\\\ {b}^{14}{c}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f951e3d-f904-57c0-a208-d0aa3eba31c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550437} {:answer/id #uuid "2420e859-faac-5e9c-aad9-2c3cc3554c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550456} {:answer/id #uuid "48563d18-51b3-56ca-8174-bcd3d2395f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550538} {:answer/id #uuid "bc408780-d5be-5c9c-a818-aa55dd20d3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550885} {:answer/id #uuid "d62ec90e-b49c-5418-9264-c77f8cc9f9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48563d18-51b3-56ca-8174-bcd3d2395f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e8f9f6-5607-5264-a7eb-d2c11970defe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1f2dd05-26d6-5c35-85a3-93dc306cbcbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-293312" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3}+1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(1-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{3}-1)}{(\\sqrt{3}+1)(\\sqrt{3}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-(1)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1-3+\\sqrt{3}}{3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-4}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "836062b0-524b-538a-ae83-7cad477cb4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052804} {:answer/id #uuid "8536fa93-92ba-5579-8423-a5d6f6b4e5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052805} {:answer/id #uuid "965dad9f-9100-51ca-9460-5f28cbd40484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052806} {:answer/id #uuid "9b99030e-5927-5114-bb6a-579ed4a631f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052807} {:answer/id #uuid "1c804d08-6d90-5c98-bbd8-0145eeb15e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8536fa93-92ba-5579-8423-a5d6f6b4e5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052805} :answer-field/id #uuid "0738cf39-8cba-5493-9162-fc131deb3619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a40f13fa-0e75-5597-9336-15bb065e38ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-41015" :question/problem "Evaluate $1+(2-3x)$ for $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We replace $x$ with $-1$ in the expression and evaluate the parentheses first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(2-3x) & = \\\\ 1+(2-3\\cdot (-1)) & = \\\\ 1+(2-(-3)) & = \\\\ 1+(2+3) & = \\\\ 1+5 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66648cb-8f77-50c9-893b-aedc4963bcc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052852} {:answer/id #uuid "b36b0693-1103-5abf-95a7-39b28db21c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052853} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa7a2bb-27be-5cfc-8be2-2abb2d0d90ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052854} {:answer/id #uuid "f546ef42-84c8-5376-84c5-ad082e37282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052855} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7aa1a8-a1e0-58a3-9255-7868dd176226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66648cb-8f77-50c9-893b-aedc4963bcc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052852} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6737b0c-55e1-56fc-bfb1-13dc14fd1b5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbc810b0-69a7-578e-ab11-1175446069d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-41369" :question/problem "${(-3)}^{3}+{4}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{3}+{4}^{3}=(-27)+64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)+64 & = \\\\ -27+64 & = \\\\ 37\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f91b176-7a76-531d-bf78-d71efd4387f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052972} {:answer/id #uuid "b92e5659-9a39-53ea-9213-d39f1ed36e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052973} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6aae1-a2f8-5c78-ab69-05c5e8cd91a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/re