swer/value "13x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445121} {:answer/id #uuid "deb897d7-a8b5-5562-a054-a53e4d9571ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40x-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "356915f5-cbce-57bf-8584-53e5840fc46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40x+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445116} :answer-field/id #uuid "be083618-5a2e-58ef-947f-78020a2cace9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6a0a35d-7b70-51dc-89d3-efb1feb64960" :question/math-academy-id "q-560" :question/problem "$5(8x+3)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(8x+3) & = \\\\ 5\\cdot 8x+5\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ 40x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb076d1-905e-50e2-a396-6f6ef860a604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x+2y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445495} {:answer/id #uuid "53d0bab1-9c5e-56e7-acb5-a6ed89ec296e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x-2y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445499} {:answer/id #uuid "81a6251e-ccc5-59a9-b49b-305692f48306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11x+5y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445505} {:answer/id #uuid "9204982f-c56c-5cf6-8e73-46bede6e1f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x-2y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445506} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9aa503-96ed-5b4e-86cc-b96db3e214bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x-2y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd9aa503-96ed-5b4e-86cc-b96db3e214bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x-2y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445517} :answer-field/id #uuid "0524c63d-c5cd-5b8d-80be-75cddeeee83b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a35d688d-c263-50c4-b62e-c79c29a24f63" :question/math-academy-id "q-563" :question/problem "$13x-2y+5x+1=$" :question/worked-solution "We rearrange the terms so that the like terms, $13x$ and $5x$, are together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13x-2y+5x+1 & = \\\\ \\underbrace{13x+5x}_{x\\text{ terms}}-2y+1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{13x+5x}-2y+1 & = \\\\ (13+5)x-2y+1 & = \\\\ 18x-2y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277311aa-fc6b-5313-ab2d-b522f597d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053730} {:answer/id #uuid "0368e135-7244-5bd0-b1eb-10f2cbb3ff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479366} {:answer/id #uuid "0f94d5f2-416f-5904-ba93-5d8d90a6e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479381} {:answer/id #uuid "85810fab-227b-5189-8c9d-62f691a1d160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(x+1)}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479557} {:answer/id #uuid "f38c5c9a-e585-55c7-a855-0be88c9f180c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f94d5f2-416f-5904-ba93-5d8d90a6e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479381} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e485d8a-d83d-55b0-a881-7cee8f9d2502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c99039b2-017b-5b94-ab7c-1d3602f59629" :question/math-academy-id "q-57766" :question/problem "$\\frac{10x}{12(x+1)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $10x$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 5\\cdot x,$ and the $12(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10x}{12(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6c82e08-3970-5a05-a527-9b764895c146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053836} {:answer/id #uuid "046f80e2-0acd-5be2-8e46-ea6e7de2f83f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053837} {:answer/id #uuid "058f57b6-69a1-5c96-a926-b724d9247e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053838} {:answer/id #uuid "11b0caef-0521-5db9-ab0e-68cefd11fa36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053839} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5c5f87-1f78-5af6-b291-64fb3352abbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c82e08-3970-5a05-a527-9b764895c146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053836} :answer-field/id #uuid "812f4b49-4a29-5638-9567-f2108887e56e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5329f7f5-2259-50ff-a14e-44701ea00a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-60566" :question/problem "Find $n$ such that  ${z}^{5}\\cdot {z}^{7}\\cdot {z}^{2}={z}^{n}$  is correct." :question/worked-solution "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{5}\\cdot {z}^{7}\\cdot {z}^{2} & = \\\\ {z}^{5+7+2} & = \\\\ {z}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore ${z}^{n}={z}^{14}$, so $n=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbf1f72-06b0-520d-bcce-fe7a87516e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054028} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed13fb1-485e-541f-8244-edff58a9edcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054029} {:answer/id #uuid "402ab3f3-62da-5651-83a1-94331abbd494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054030} {:answer/id #uuid "5d896cad-d52b-57a0-9b03-cd6b8039620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054031} {:answer/id #uuid "601b00f5-8338-5dfb-9271-e19b674aab25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d896cad-d52b-57a0-9b03-cd6b8039620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054031} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf83468-7688-50ec-b222-9820046725f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a406bc65-b6be-5e0c-9457-9bc53f06a1e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-68933" :question/problem "Evaluate $(-\\frac{1}{3})÷((-4)^{3}÷8).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, consider the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3}÷8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3}÷8 & = \\\\ (-64)÷8 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-64)}{8} & = \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{3})÷((-4)^{3}÷8) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{3})÷(-8) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{3})\\times (-\\frac{1}{8}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 1}{3\\times 8} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059471 :question/id #uuid "9ee1e04e-8a36-5597-8928-c2ebd67c1b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3139" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & = \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059604 :question/id #uuid "8369943c-d7d8-5b77-949b-b48a4a4a96d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5780" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac {4 + \\textrm{i}} {1 - \\textrm{i}}$ in the form $a+\\textrm{i}b.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $1-\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $1+\\textrm{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1+\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4 + \\textrm{i}} {1 - \\textrm{i}} \n&=\\dfrac {4 + \\textrm{i}} {1 - \\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {1 + \\textrm{i}} {1 + \\textrm{i}}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {4 + 4\\textrm{i} + \\textrm{i} + \\textrm{i}^2} {1^2 - \\textrm{i}^2}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {4 + 5\\textrm{i} + (-1)} {1 - (-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {3 + 5\\textrm{i}} {2}\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+\\textrm{i} b \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3 + 5\\textrm{i}} {2} =\n\t\t\\dfrac{3} {2} + \\dfrac {5} {2} \\textrm{i}\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059605 :question/id #uuid "107d76c0-349a-549d-adeb-f009492cd79c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5781" :question/problem "If $z_1 = -8-3\\textrm{i}$ and $z_2 = 2\\textrm{i},$ then what is $z_1$ divided by $z_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {-8 - 3\\textrm{i}} {2\\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is the imaginary number $2\\textrm{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+2\\textrm{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-2\\textrm{i},$ or simply $-2\\textrm{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-2\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {-8 - 3\\textrm{i}} {2\\textrm{i}} \n&= \t\\dfrac {-8 - 3\\textrm{i}} {2\\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {-2\\textrm{i}} {-2\\textrm{i}} \\[5pt]\n&= \t\\dfrac {16\\textrm{i} + 6\\textrm{i}^2} {-4\\textrm{i}^2} \\[5pt]\n&= \t\\dfrac {16\\textrm{i} + 6(-1)} {-4(-1)} \\[5pt]\n&= \t\\dfrac {-6 + 16\\textrm{i}} {4}\t\t\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {-6 + 16\\textrm{i}} {4} &= - \\dfrac{6}{4} + \\dfrac{16}{4} \\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n&=\t\t-\\dfrac{3} {2} + 4\\textrm{i}\t\t\t\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059606 :question/id #uuid "74740363-2309-5680-9373-05ce0fed9bcf" :question/math-academy-id "e-6230" :question/problem "If $z_1=3\\textrm{i}$ and $z_2 = 2+5\\textrm{i},$ then what is $z_1$ divided by $z_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\textrm{i}}{2+5\\textrm i}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $2+5\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $2-5\\textrm{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $2-5\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3 \\textrm{i}} {2 +5\\textrm{i}} \n&= \\dfrac {3 \\textrm{i}} {2 +5 \\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {2 -5 \\textrm{i}} {2-5\\textrm{i}}  \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15 \\textrm{i}^2 } {2^2 - (5\\textrm{i})^2}  \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15\\textrm{i}^2 } {4 - 25\\textrm{i}^2} \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15(-1) } {4 - 25(-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} + 15 } {29}\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} + 15 } {29}=\n\t\t\\dfrac{15} {29} + \\dfrac {6} {29} \\textrm{i}\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059607 :question/id #uuid "2fc036dd-7732-50ed-adef-8c37bd87fecd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6231" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{4}{-1+2\\textrm{i} }$ in the form $a+\\textrm{i}b.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $-1+2\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $-1-2\\textrm{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-1-2\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4} {-1 +2\\textrm{i}} \n&=\\dfrac {4} {-1 +2 \\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {-1 -2 \\textrm{i}} {-1-2\\textrm{i}}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {(-1)^2 - (2\\textrm{i})^2}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {1 - 4\\textrm{i}^2}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {1 - 4(-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {5} \n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {5} =\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4} {5} - \\dfrac {8} {5} \\textrm{i}\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063919 :question/id #uuid "46a52c71-86ed-5e9c-a1cb-f2110a4c6484" :question/math-academy-id "e-1073" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{x^2}{x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ {x}^{(2-3)} & = \\\\ {x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we convert the negative exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{x^3} = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063920 :question/id #uuid "d09010dc-a725-5d22-91ba-cb4372187dbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-1074" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{z^5}{z^{-3} }.$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{5}}{{z}^{-3}} & = \\\\ {z}^{5-(-3)} & = \\\\ {z}^{5+3} & = \\\\ {z}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063921 :question/id #uuid "a8d19b4c-5361-5520-b0c9-7f6667b6a1c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1075" :question/problem "Simplify the  expression $\\dfrac {p^7} {p^2}  \\cdot p^3 .$" :question/worked-solution "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{7}}{{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{(7-2)}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{5}\\cdot {p}^{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{p}^{5}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{(5+3)} & = \\\\ {p}^{8}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{7}}{{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3}={p}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063937 :question/id #uuid "46597138-36cc-584b-ade8-70cbfafcad2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12905" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4x = y$ for the variable $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nIn this case, we simply divide both sides of the equation by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\t4x &= y \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {4x} {4} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {{\\cancel{4}}x} {\\cancel{4}} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac y4\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063947 :question/id #uuid "78675372-1e29-5088-8907-52d9e54124e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13083" :question/problem "What is $\\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 4\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 7\\right)}$ expressed as a single fraction?" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division.  So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{7}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{7}{3}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{7}{12} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a positive number times a negative number gives a negative number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063948 :question/id #uuid "3bad12f4-14b2-5ab6-a1db-9d460b9a26ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-13084" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-3)}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}=-3÷(-\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $-3 =- \\dfrac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{5}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{5}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{1})÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{1})\\times (-\\frac{5}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{3} & = \\\\ 5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)} = 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a negative number times a negative number gives a positive number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063949 :question/id #uuid "87974d27-c494-5b1a-84e9-03e39f3e0b1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13086" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{3}{2})÷6 & = \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{6}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3}{12} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063953 :question/id #uuid "e0ffd89f-c43e-578d-a2b0-da1229a4ef0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1323" :question/problem "Factor $3t^2+12tw$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(3t^2,12tw) = 3t\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3t^{2} + 12tw &=  \\\\\n&= (3t \\cdot t) + (3t \\cdot 4w)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3t \\cdot t) + (3t \\cdot 4w) &=  \\\\\n&= 3t(t + 4w)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063954 :question/id #uuid "7cbc7f2d-a2df-5382-bff4-d8a7e10c9b6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1324" :question/problem "Factor $15x^2-5x-25xy$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of the three terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(15x^2, 5x, 25xy) = 5x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15x^{2} - 5x - 25xy &=  \\\\\n&= (5x \\cdot 3x) - (5x \\cdot 1) - (5x \\cdot 5y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5x \\cdot 3x) - (5x \\cdot 1) - (5x \\cdot 5y) &=  \\\\\n&= 5x(3x - 1 - 5y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063955 :question/id #uuid "8b52d41f-e7f4-54e8-844a-1881256fbd54" :question/math-academy-id "e-1325" :question/problem "Factor the polynomial expression $8a(b^2-3)-c(b^2-3)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(8a(b^2-3),c(b^2-3)) = b^2-3\n$$\n\nEach term is already written as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n\n$$\n8a \\cdot (b^{2} - 3) - c \\cdot (b^{2} - 3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a \\cdot (b^{2} - 3) - c \\cdot (b^{2} - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (8a - c)(b^{2} - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063956 :question/id #uuid "18486b46-10bc-5e64-b1d9-df3ba0736b05" :question/math-academy-id "e-13262" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $0-(-6).$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-6$ from $0,$ we combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-(-6) & = \\\\ 0+6 & = \\\\ 6 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/a7/a793deb0faa2f175a7f9bddc13dcff381b48aaee40b6082bd320a8748d8c8c37.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063957 :question/id #uuid "1c75cfa3-630e-5b37-82c0-ec421bb8c206" :question/math-academy-id "e-13263" :question/problem "Subtract $-100$ from $-85$." :question/worked-solution "To subtract $−100$ from $-85,$ we combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-85-(-100) & = \\\\ -85+100 & = \\\\ 15 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063969 :question/id #uuid "3b855590-1e40-5dee-a518-0211c627281e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13909" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $2x - y = 5y - z.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $x,$ so we will treat the other variables $y$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x.$\n\nFirst, we add $y$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x - y &= 5y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x - y + y &= 5y - z + y \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2x} 2 &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2} \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2}\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063984 :question/id #uuid "7361c770-5274-5ed2-8366-e4b7ba21ff9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14301" :question/problem "Simplify $-3t^3\\left(3t^2+2t\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t) & = \\\\ (-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t) & = \\\\ ((-3)\\cdot 3)({t}^{3}\\cdot {t}^{2})+((-3)\\cdot 2)({t}^{3}\\cdot t) & = \\\\ (-9)({t}^{3+2})+(-6)({t}^{3+1}) & = \\\\ (-9)({t}^{5})+(-6)({t}^{4}) & = \\\\ -9{t}^{5}-6{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063985 :question/id #uuid "e8968548-b5a7-55c9-af2e-48aecb217cc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14304" :question/problem "Simplify $-5x\\left(x^3-4\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x({x}^{3}-4) & = \\\\ (-5x)({x}^{3})+(-5x)(-4) & = \\\\ (-5)(x\\cdot {x}^{3})+((-5)\\cdot (-4))(x) & = \\\\ (-5){x}^{1+3}+(20)x & = \\\\ -5{x}^{4}+20x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063986 :question/id #uuid "0317d27d-9501-5de5-a55a-b9a73ce39500" :question/math-academy-id "e-14306" :question/problem "Simplify $-6x^2\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) & = \\\\ -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063987 :question/id #uuid "7e3162ad-ed76-548d-a020-142ea0c0976b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14309" :question/problem "Simplify $xy\\left(3x^2+xy+4\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy(3{x}^{2}+xy+4) & = \\\\ (xy)\\cdot (3{x}^{2})+(xy)\\cdot (xy)+(xy)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ 3(x\\cdot {x}^{2})(y)+(x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y)+4(xy) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3})(y)+({x}^{2})({y}^{2})+4xy & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4xy & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063988 :question/id #uuid "ab6787e7-5b47-51a6-8967-4ede296cc44f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14313" :question/problem "Expand and simplify $(y + 5)(y - 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(y + 5)(y - 2) &= \\\n\t\ty(y-2) + 5(y-2) &=\\\n\t\ty^2-2y +5y-10 &=\\\n\t\ty^2 + 3y - 10\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063989 :question/id #uuid "818a9bf5-5ed5-5809-a295-3d332ce98b47" :question/math-academy-id "e-14316" :question/problem "Expand and simplify $(2n - 1)(3n - 6).$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2n - 1)(3n - 6) &= \\\n\t\t2n(3n - 6) + (-1)(3n - 6) &= \\\n\t\t6n^2 - 12n - 3n + 6 &= \\\n\t\t6n^2 - 15n + 6\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063990 :question/id #uuid "4e99bb73-42a1-5b11-8463-020f297c5afc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14317" :question/problem "Expand $(p^2-q^2)(2p+3q).$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}-{q}^{2})(2p+3q) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}(2p+3q)+(-{q}^{2})(2p+3q) & = \\\\ 2{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}q-2p{q}^{2}-3{q}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063991 :question/id #uuid "70f19ea4-d0b8-537a-8ae4-06a236cf37cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-14318" :question/problem "Expand $(4+3y)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=3y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(4 + 3y)^2 &= \\\n\t\t(4)^2 + 2(4) ( 3y) + (3y)^2 &= \\\n\t\t16 + 24y + 9y^2.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063992 :question/id #uuid "c1a6df9e-b1e8-51c8-b5df-4cc624e14193" :question/math-academy-id "e-14319" :question/problem "Expand $(2n^2 + 3)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2n^2$ and $b=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2n^2 + 3)^2 &= \\[2pt]\n\t\t(2n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) ( 3) + (3)^2 &= \\[2pt]\n\t\t2^2\\cdot (n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) (3) + (3)^2 &= \\[2pt]\n\t\t4n^4 + 12n^2 + 9.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063993 :question/id #uuid "487f1c4c-63c9-51cf-9a0e-7d9bc0a4c7c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14320" :question/problem "Expand $(3p - 5)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3p$ and $b=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(3p - 5)^2 &= \\\n\t\t(3p)^2 - 2(3p)(5) + 5^2 &= \\\n\t\t9p^2 - 30p + 25.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064002 :question/id #uuid "44cf8c85-f228-5eaa-922d-613355cb7d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15070" :question/problem "Factor the quadratic expression $10a^2 + 11a + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n10a^2 + 11a + 3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10 \\times 3 = 30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($11$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30), \\quad (2,15), \\quad (3,10), \\quad (5,6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,6)$ add to $11{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+6 & =11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5a + 6a,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{a}^{2}+11a+3 & = \\\\ 10{a}^{2}+5a+6a+3 & = \\\\ (10{a}^{2}+5a)+(6a+3) & = \\\\ 5a(2a+1)+3(2a+1) & = \\\\ (5a+3)(2a+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :question/id #uuid "ad032833-ecf8-5295-9a9f-f69f759c19b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-15072" :question/problem "Factor the expression $3x^2 - 16x + 16.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 16x + 16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3 \\times 16 = 48$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-16$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $48.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $48$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-48), \\quad (-2,-24), \\quad (-3,-16), \\quad (-4,-12), \\quad (-6,-8).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-12)$ add to $-16{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)+(-12) & =-16\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4x - 12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-16x+16 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x-12x+16 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-4x)-(12x-16) & = \\\\ x(3x-4)-4(3x-4) & = \\\\ (x-4)(3x-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :question/id #uuid "35bedd9f-bcb7-5024-bfe7-5ef6d34518bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-15073" :question/problem "Factor the quadratic expression $2c^2 + 15c - 8.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2c^2 + 15c - 8.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times -8 = -16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-16{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\quad (-1,16), \\quad (2,-8), \\quad (-2,8), \\quad (4,-4)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n(-1) + 16 = 15 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $- c+16c,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}^{2}+15c-8 & = \\\\ 2{c}^{2}-c+16c-8 & = \\\\ (2{c}^{2}-c)+(16c-8) & = \\\\ c(2c-1)+8(2c-1) & = \\\\ (2c-1)(c+8) & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :question/id #uuid "d8b425b2-eb7b-5cef-b85d-56a67a421d6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1594" :question/problem "Factor the expression $3x^2-300$." :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $3$. Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n3x^2-300 = 3(x^2-100)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 100 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - (10)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 10)(x - 10)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n3(x^2-100) = 3(x+10)(x-10)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064006 :question/id #uuid "c4a15443-e2a4-5bda-ab14-8fb427ec6ca4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1784" :question/problem "Express $-49s^2 + 100r^2$ as a product of two binomials." :question/worked-solution "We can write this as a difference of squares by switching the order of the terms. Then, we factor as usual.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-49s^{2} + 100r^{2} &=  \\\\\n100r^{2} - 49s^{2} &=  \\\\\n(10r)^{2} - (7s)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (10r - 7s)(10r + 7s)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064008 :question/id #uuid "ad4e086f-9f68-5177-a4ed-e12bb986d7a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1799" :question/problem "Factor $4x^2+10x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(4x^2,10x) = 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\textrm{GCF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} + 10x &=  \\\\\n&= (2x \\cdot 2x) + (2x \\cdot 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\textrm{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x \\cdot 2x) + (2x \\cdot 5) &=  \\\\\n&= 2x(2x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064046 :question/id #uuid "a0f47cd2-ea8f-5278-ab67-46ecd65d2f4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3737" :question/problem "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](images/74/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064049 :question/id #uuid "3ff03590-c618-5a60-b2ac-e0a20be6a8ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-377" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6e^x  \\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring out the constant $6$ and taking the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 6e^x   \\textrm d x   =6 \\int e^x  \\, \\textrm d x =6e^x +C.\n                                                                               \n}\n$$"}]}
